{% aside type="caution" title="In development" %}
Fine-tuning options and defaults may change between milu releases. Run
`milu --help` for the options of your installed version.
{% /aside %}

milu can train a new model from scratch or fine-tune an existing model
package on your own data. Fine-tuning starts from a model that has already
learned general features, such as an ImageNet-pretrained ResNet, and adapts it
to a smaller, specialized dataset, such as photos of specimens from one
collection.

- **Train from scratch:** leave `base_model` unset. milu builds a new network
  from `architecture`, for example a multilayer perceptron for measurements.
- **Fine-tune:** set `base_model` to a model package. The architecture comes
  from that package, and `architecture` is ignored.

## Strategies

The `strategy` setting controls which layers are trained:

| Strategy           | What is trained                                                  | Needs a base model |
| ------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------- | :----------------: |
| `full`             | Every layer                                                      |         No         |
| `head_only`        | Only the head; the backbone is frozen                            |        Yes         |
| `gradual_unfreeze` | Only the head for the first half of the epochs, then every layer |        Yes         |

`milu train init` picks `head_only` when you pass a base model and `full`
otherwise. Head-only training is fast and works with little data.
Gradual unfreezing adapts the whole network while protecting the pretrained
features early in training.

## Fine-tune an image model

{% steps %}

1. Download a pretrained model.

   ```bash
   milu models pull resnet18
   ```

   Run `milu models list` to see the available models. To use a torchvision or
   timm checkpoint you already have, convert it with `milu models import`.

2. Write a training config.

   The input can be an image folder with one sub-directory per class, or an
   extracted NAHPU data package. For a data package, `--rank` chooses the
   taxon rank used as the label.

   ```bash
   milu train init -i nahpu_package/ --rank genus --base-model out_models/resnet18 -n genus_id
   ```

3. Review `milu_train.toml`.

   Adjust the strategy, epochs, or learning rate. See the
   [configuration reference](#configuration-reference) below.

4. Train.

   ```bash
   milu train run
   ```

   The model package is written to `out_models/genus_id`.

5. Evaluate on data the model has not seen.

   ```bash
   milu test -m out_models/genus_id -i holdout_package/ --rank genus
   ```

{% /steps %}

Tabular models follow the same steps, starting from a CSV or TSV file:

```bash
milu train init -i measurements.csv -t genus --id-column catalog -n skull_genus
```

## Configuration reference

`milu_train.toml` has three sections. A relative dataset path is resolved
against the config's directory, so you can move a config together with its
data.

### `[dataset]`

| `type`         | Settings                                                      |
| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `csv`          | `path`, `target_column`, optional `id_column` and `feature_columns` (all other columns when unset) |
| `image_folder` | `path`                                                        |
| `nahpu`        | `path`, `rank`, optional media category                       |

### `[model]`

| Setting          | Default       | Description                                                       |
| ---------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `name`           | `model`       | Name written to the model manifest                                |
| `task`           | classification | What the model predicts                                          |
| `architecture`   | none          | Architecture for a new model; ignored with `base_model`           |
| `base_model`     | none          | Model package to start from                                       |
| `strategy`       | `full`        | Which layers are trained (see [Strategies](#strategies))          |
| `epochs`         | 50            | Maximum number of passes over the training data                   |
| `batch_size`     | 32            | Samples per optimizer step                                        |
| `learning_rate`  | 0.001         | AdamW learning rate                                               |
| `weight_decay`   | 0.0001        | AdamW decoupled weight decay                                      |
| `valid_fraction` | 0.15          | Fraction held out for early stopping                              |
| `test_fraction`  | 0.15          | Fraction held out for the final test report                       |
| `seed`           | 42            | Seed for splitting, shuffling, and weight initialization          |
| `patience`       | 10            | Stop after this many epochs without a lower validation loss; unset trains every epoch |
| `augment`        | `true`        | Randomly crop, flip, and color-jitter training images             |
| `image_size`     | 224           | Input side length for new image models; fine-tuning uses the base model's size |

### `[output]`

`formats` lists the weight formats to write: `burnpack`, `safetensors`, or
both. The first one goes in the main package directory. You can also choose
them on the command line:

```bash
milu train run -c milu_train.toml --formats burnpack,safetensors
```

## Results

milu keeps the model from the epoch with the lowest validation loss (or
training loss, without a validation split). The run reports the loss of every
epoch, how the data was split, and metrics on the test split. Use
`milu models info` to check what a package contains before you share it.
